\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Variable discriminability (TVD) across FFS interfaces for the top-20 ranked variables. Rows are sorted by maximum TVD across all interfaces; columns show FFS interfaces $\lambda _0$--$\lambda _4$. Cell color and annotation give TVD between the reach-$B$ and fail distributions at that interface. Most variables peak at $\lambda _0$, indicating the initial environment is the strongest predictor of eventual genesis. MSLP~attr (the FFS order parameter attribution field) peaks at $\lambda _3$--$\lambda _4$, as expected.}}{35}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tvd_heatmap}{{9}{35}{Variable discriminability (TVD) across FFS interfaces for the top-20 ranked variables. Rows are sorted by maximum TVD across all interfaces; columns show FFS interfaces $\lambda _0$--$\lambda _4$. Cell color and annotation give TVD between the reach-$B$ and fail distributions at that interface. Most variables peak at $\lambda _0$, indicating the initial environment is the strongest predictor of eventual genesis. MSLP~attr (the FFS order parameter attribution field) peaks at $\lambda _3$--$\lambda _4$, as expected}{figure.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Late-emerging variable discriminability: top-20 variables ranked by TVD gain (max TVD at $\lambda _2$+ minus early peak TVD). Positive gain indicates a variable becomes \textit  {more} discriminating as the storm develops, revealing predictors that are invisible at early interfaces but informative at depth. The MSLP attribution field dominates at $\lambda _3$--$\lambda _4$; mid-level winds and upper-level divergence indicators emerge as secondary late-stage predictors.}}{36}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tvd_heatmap_late}{{10}{36}{Late-emerging variable discriminability: top-20 variables ranked by TVD gain (max TVD at $\lambda _2$+ minus early peak TVD). Positive gain indicates a variable becomes \textit {more} discriminating as the storm develops, revealing predictors that are invisible at early interfaces but informative at depth. The MSLP attribution field dominates at $\lambda _3$--$\lambda _4$; mid-level winds and upper-level divergence indicators emerge as secondary late-stage predictors}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Conditional transition probabilities $P(\lambda _i|\lambda _{i-1})$ for all 98 initial conditions (rows, chronological order) and four shooting interfaces (columns). Color: probability from low (red) to high (green). Three case-study ICs (Earl~09-02, active~09-09, Ian~09-22) are outlined in black. \textit  {Bottom panel}: season-mean conditional probabilities with $\pm 1\sigma $ range; the ``season bottleneck'' bar marks the interface with the smallest mean probability. The first interface $P(\lambda _1|\lambda _0)$ is most variable across the season, while $P(\lambda _4|\lambda _3)$ is systematically the smallest probability — the universal final-intensification barrier.}}{37}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bottleneck_heatmap}{{11}{37}{Conditional transition probabilities $P(\lambda _i|\lambda _{i-1})$ for all 98 initial conditions (rows, chronological order) and four shooting interfaces (columns). Color: probability from low (red) to high (green). Three case-study ICs (Earl~09-02, active~09-09, Ian~09-22) are outlined in black. \textit {Bottom panel}: season-mean conditional probabilities with $\pm 1\sigma $ range; the ``season bottleneck'' bar marks the interface with the smallest mean probability. The first interface $P(\lambda _1|\lambda _0)$ is most variable across the season, while $P(\lambda _4|\lambda _3)$ is systematically the smallest probability — the universal final-intensification barrier}{figure.11}{}}
